analysis.could_contextdb_be_built_as_plugin_on_it:
Partly, and it is feasible. ContextDB could use Dolt (sql-server, or embedded through the Go driver) as its storage and versioning substrate:
- items, revisions, dependency edges and certificate tables are ordinary SQL tables;
- a Dolt commit or branch gives snapshots and what-if alternatives;
- dolt_merge gives cell-level 3-way merges of agent branches;
- dolt_conflicts and dolt_constraint_violations give PENDING-like blockers;
- AS OF and dolt_diff give certified-read snapshots and change feeds for invalidation.

The justification DAG, support counting, flip-only propagation, semantic validation and action gating would live in a ContextDB service layer. That layer runs propagation after each merge or commit (e.g. diff against the previous head via dolt_diff), and does action gating as a SQL transaction that re-reads certificate dependencies before committing.

Limitations:
- There is no pre-commit veto hook in Dolt itself. Gating must happen in ContextDB's transaction logic, which can use the merge-on-commit retry.
- Dolt's single-root CAS serializes every ref update.
- It is a heavy dependency: a Go binary running a MySQL server.
- Whole-DB branches don't map cleanly onto scoped alternatives.

A lighter path is to borrow the designs (content-addressed revisions, multi-head CAS with preconditions, the DiffOp merge taxonomy, artifacts) and implement them on SQLite.

analysis.overlap_with_contextdb:
Neither project does dependency-driven transitive invalidation, alternative-support (OR) justification, support counting, or action gating.

agent-memory has:
- immutable-ish old revisions kept as files;
- a supersede pointer (superseded_by) plus validity intervals;
- as_of history reads;
- evidence provenance into an append-only archive;
- a global flock;
- a content-hash optimistic check, used internally only and not exposed via MCP.

Its "links" are untyped and are validated against active targets only at write time. Superseding or deleting a target never touches dependents; they only show up as dangling links after gc. Its invariant 7 explicitly rejects any third state between active and invalid, which is the opposite of ContextDB's PENDING.

deja-vu has:
- manual decision lifecycle states (accepted/rejected/superseded/stale), the closest thing to a semantic-validity state, but set by hand per session and never propagated;
- a lexical-overlap 'superseded' heuristic;
- a git-based 'files changed since' staleness annotation, the closest thing to a Cur/Pin dependency on code, computed lazily at read time and advisory;
- a PreToolUse hook that injects one prior decision but never blocks.

Neither has logical heads with CAS, certificates, a justification DAG, or WHY/WHY_NOT.
